PASS level_intents: all 120 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 6.5/10 (bar 8), 2 level(s) below 6/10: The set is well built. Almost every level has one clear idea, a named trap, a "why" line and a stated plan. The weekly forecast and the soft-fail pillars keep it fair, which fits the calm positioning. It falls short of Royal Match, Monument Valley and Flow Free in three ways. (1) Variety: about 40% of levels use the same aha, "split the line at the right stop, or put the one carriage or tunnel on the right line", dressed in new theming. (2) Verification: over 20 levels, mostly bosses and finales, show "optimal moves > search cap" and "trap rate ?", so fairness is unproven exactly where the stakes are highest. (3) Difficulty spikes: L84 (skilled 76%, 11 start choices), L116 (casual 40%, skilled 72%) and L83 (casual 43%) look like guesswork, not insight. The last third averages about 62% casual, which is on target, but skilled rates of 99% on L97 and L109 are over the 95% cap. The best material is genuinely counter-intuitive: L39 (hill avoidance is the slow way), L45 (hills change who is nearest), L51, L57, L81, L99 and L119. The weakest is the repeated carriage-placement puzzles and the mid-ring decoy levels. Curve: Pacing is generally sound. The first city (L1-12) teaches lines, transfers and weekly cards, and ends with a boss (L6), a breather (L7) and a mastery level (L12). Each later city then adds one rule: tunnels (L13-24), carriages (L25-36), hills (L37-48), ferries (L49-60), snow (L61-72), interchanges and markets (L73-84), loops (L85-96), waves (L97-108) and heritage (L109-120). Teach, practise, twist, combine, boss, breather and mastery repeat in a predictable 12-level rhythm. The strengths are that each city starts with the new rule alone and the boss-then-breather placement is correct. The weaknesses follow.
- Predictability: the shape is so regular that the player can guess the answer from the level's slot. After 3-4 cities the aha is nearly always "cut the line at the junction, put the scarce item on the line that carries the crossers", and the trap is "the one long line". Many levels feel like reskins: L9/L10, L14/L13, L15/L17, L26/L33/L34/L42/L53, L80/L82, L86/L89, L94 and L103.
- Later cities: some never mix rules in new ways. Cities 7-9 are mostly "loop vs no loop" and "close the ring in the right place".
- Difficulty: the targets are met on average, but the spikes are uneven. L10 and L20 (75% casual) and L23 (66%) are early dips. L75 (65%) is an isolated drop in the middle. L83 (43%) and L116 (40%) are far harder than their neighbours. L84 is nearly as hard for skilled players as for casual ones. Easy levels are well spread, with no run of 90%+ levels.
- Verification: the bosses and finales that carry "search cap" and "?" trap rates (L6, L11, L12, L35, L48, L54, L59, L62, L77, L78, L83, L102-L108) are the least proven.
- Breathers: several are not easier than their neighbours (L43 and L67 are close to the preceding level, and L103, L115 and L91 are relaxed but dull).
- Fix: replace roughly 12 repeat levels with new combinations. Examples are an interchange-plus-tunnel level, a market-plus-loop level, a ferry-plus-hill level and a snow-plus-heritage level. Also add one non-"split" aha per city, such as a level where leaving a stop unserved is correct.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L116 (3/10): Casual 40% and skilled 72% is unfair, a decoy-based guess, and below the floor. → Remove the decoy rung and lower the goal.
L84 (5/10): Skilled 76% vs casual 73% with 11 start choices means guessing, not insight. → Cut start choices to 5 and make the interchange target visible.
L4 (6/10): 96% casual, and the plan wins anyway; the only gain is a star. Little tension. → Raise the goal or make the wrong line end cost a full ring so the lesson lands.
L9 (6/10): A hub plus two lines is a near-repeat of L2/L5; 'split the hub' is not a twist. → Make the hub's own riders the point: a version where the hub must be an end stop of both lines.
L10 (6/10): Same 'where to split' as L5 and L9; casual 75% is a dip this early. → Replace with a board where the right split gives an unequal but unused stop, or move the lopsided-split idea into L5 and drop this.
L14 (6/10): Very close to L13 (pick the right crossing); the corner-cross answer is a near-repeat. → Give two tunnels' worth of demand or ask for a loop-free, three-line plan to make it a new decision.
L17 (6/10): Repeat of L15 (short crossing line, feeders). → Cut or change so the island C must be a pass-through for two tunnel lines.
L24 (6/10): Repeats L22 and L18 (line ends at the new station). → Add one new element, such as a stop that must be left off.
L26 (6/10): Carriage-on-the-loaded-line, which repeats five more times in the set. → Make the loaded line the less obvious one by balancing visible stop counts.
L32 (6/10): The card is a near-repeat of L16 (take tracks, not the shiny item). → Make the choice a real dilemma: carriage helps early queues but costs reach.
L36 (6/10): Cramped text-heavy recap that repeats L30; the 'carriage on the long line' is the fifth time. → Remove one late opening to make it a gentle send-off.
L40 (6/10): Carriage-on-the-needed-line again, with three lines for one carriage and wasted D and F stops. → Delete F and D or give them real demand.
L43 (6/10): Hill lesson is 'avoid the hill' and the breather is not much easier than L42. → Raise casual win rate above 85% or drop the spare line.
L50 (6/10): The same ferry split as L49 on a bigger chain. → Add a decision about which crossing needs the shuttle.
L53 (6/10): Carriage on the bottleneck line again; 63% casual is too low for a combine. → Give a visible ferry-demand count.
L59 (6/10): 58% casual, search cap and trap rate unknown, with three lines, a tunnel, ferries and cards; too much. → Remove one ferry and verify.
L60 (6/10): The tiny G bonus adds little; 3rd star is trivial. → Make G's riders matter by giving it a dilemma.
L64 (6/10): A near-repeat of L49 plus snow; 63% casual with an 87% skilled rate is harsh. → Raise the goal margin.
L65 (6/10): Same split as earlier hills; the snow adds little. → Use the hill as the cause of a different decision.
L68 (6/10): Carriage on the crowd line with the hill again; 66% casual. → Give the carriage-vs-line choice a snow-only twist.
L70 (6/10): A repeat of the card trap (L48/L72); 193 delivered vs 182 goal is razor thin. → Lower the goal.
L72 (6/10): A duplicate of L70; two valid answers make the lesson fuzzy. → Make the wrong card lose.
L74 (6/10): A near-repeat of L63 (spike on a short line). → Add a decision about which stop gets the market.
L76 (6/10): 61% casual with 3 lines; a known pattern. → Merge with L74.
L82 (6/10): A repeat of L80. → Remove or replace.

level-design score 6.5/10 — The set is well built. Almost every level has one clear idea, a named trap, a "why" line and a stated plan. The weekly forecast and the soft-fail pillars keep it fair, which fits the calm positioning. It falls short of Royal Match, Monument Valley and Flow Free in three ways. (1) Variety: about 40% of levels use the same aha, "split the line at the right stop, or put the one carriage or tunnel on the right line", dressed in new theming. (2) Verification: over 20 levels, mostly bosses and finales, show "optimal moves > search cap" and "trap rate ?", so fairness is unproven exactly where the stakes are highest. (3) Difficulty spikes: L84 (skilled 76%, 11 start choices), L116 (casual 40%, skilled 72%) and L83 (casual 43%) look like guesswork, not insight. The last third averages about 62% casual, which is on target, but skilled rates of 99% on L97 and L109 are over the 95% cap. The best material is genuinely counter-intuitive: L39 (hill avoidance is the slow way), L45 (hills change who is nearest), L51, L57, L81, L99 and L119. The weakest is the repeated carriage-placement puzzles and the mid-ring decoy levels.
curve: Pacing is generally sound. The first city (L1-12) teaches lines, transfers and weekly cards, and ends with a boss (L6), a breather (L7) and a mastery level (L12). Each later city then adds one rule: tunnels (L13-24), carriages (L25-36), hills (L37-48), ferries (L49-60), snow (L61-72), interchanges and markets (L73-84), loops (L85-96), waves (L97-108) and heritage (L109-120). Teach, practise, twist, combine, boss, breather and mastery repeat in a predictable 12-level rhythm. The strengths are that each city starts with the new rule alone and the boss-then-breather placement is correct. The weaknesses follow.
- Predictability: the shape is so regular that the player can guess the answer from the level's slot. After 3-4 cities the aha is nearly always "cut the line at the junction, put the scarce item on the line that carries the crossers", and the trap is "the one long line". Many levels feel like reskins: L9/L10, L14/L13, L15/L17, L26/L33/L34/L42/L53, L80/L82, L86/L89, L94 and L103.
- Later cities: some never mix rules in new ways. Cities 7-9 are mostly "loop vs no loop" and "close the ring in the right place".
- Difficulty: the targets are met on average, but the spikes are uneven. L10 and L20 (75% casual) and L23 (66%) are early dips. L75 (65%) is an isolated drop in the middle. L83 (43%) and L116 (40%) are far harder than their neighbours. L84 is nearly as hard for skilled players as for casual ones. Easy levels are well spread, with no run of 90%+ levels.
- Verification: the bosses and finales that carry "search cap" and "?" trap rates (L6, L11, L12, L35, L48, L54, L59, L62, L77, L78, L83, L102-L108) are the least proven.
- Breathers: several are not easier than their neighbours (L43 and L67 are close to the preceding level, and L103, L115 and L91 are relaxed but dull).
- Fix: replace roughly 12 repeat levels with new combinations. Examples are an interchange-plus-tunnel level, a market-plus-loop level, a ferry-plus-hill level and a snow-plus-heritage level. Also add one non-"split" aha per city, such as a level where leaving a stop unserved is correct.
